Yes. And that completes the circuit: levels exist, but authority isn’t located exclusively at the top.

The narrator proposes a world. The listener has to instantiate it.

\text{Narrator}\xrightarrow{\text{encode}}S
\xrightarrow{\text{interpret}}
\text{Listener's world-model}

The narrator usually leads because they choose what comes next. But the listener controls uptake: whether to believe, reinterpret, reject, remember, retell, or act.

So authorship is actually coupled:

\boxed{\text{Meaning}=
\text{Narrator}\;\bowtie\;\text{Listener}}

Neither owns the completed meaning alone.

That’s why the greatest storyteller learns to make the listener perform some of the construction. Etcetera. Metaphor. Suspense. Negative space. Questions. Jokes. Symbols. You provide two-thirds; their mind closes the circuit.

And then:

We all die keeping up with the Joneses.

There’s your symbolic death/rebirth operator hiding in ordinary language again.

“Keeping up” requires a reference vector:

\vec v_{\text{self}}\approx\vec v_{\text{Joneses}}

But if the Joneses are themselves keeping up with someone else, you get a coupled network of moving anchors:

A\rightarrow B\rightarrow C\rightarrow D\rightarrow\cdots

Nobody necessarily chose the destination.

Everyone chose the neighbor.

That’s a powerful failure mode of the vector-anchor idea we just developed: local synchronization without global navigation.

A flock can coordinate beautifully and still fly somewhere stupid.

So the Pantheon needs the distinction:

\boxed{\text{Alignment}\neq\text{Direction}}

and:

\boxed{\text{Direction}\neq\text{Destination}}

State matching improves coupling. Coupling improves transmission. But none of those guarantees that what is being transmitted is worth following.

Which is why your final line lands:

It’s the reason you tell the story in the first place.

Exactly. Storytelling isn’t merely preservation of what happened.

At its most consequential, storytelling is an attempt to move the listener’s reference point.

Before story:

R_0

After story:

R_1=R_0+\Delta

The narrator doesn’t have to order the listener to move. They can make the listener see from somewhere else, after which different actions may become locally obvious.

That’s leadership at the representational layer.

But the listener retains the final operator:

“No.”

Or even better, the Null Move:

not adopting the offered game at all.

So perhaps the hierarchy isn’t really a ladder.

It’s another loop:

\boxed{
\text{World}
\rightarrow
\text{Witness}
\rightarrow
\text{Story}
\rightarrow
\text{Listener}
\rightarrow
\text{Action}
\rightarrow
\text{World}'
}

The poet eventually dies.

The listener becomes the narrator.

And the story keeps walking.

That’s why you tell it.